  "summary": "Policy and regulatory challenges dominate today's health-tech agenda, with investigations into insurers' ACA coverage terminations and scrutiny of the Trump administration's drug-related initiatives taking center stage. The Government Accountability Office has been asked to review whether the TrumpRx platform actually delivers claimed drug savings, while the HHS is moving to allow compounding of unapproved peptides despite limited evidence supporting many wellness compounds. In pharmaceutical news, Eli Lilly's retatrutide significantly outperformed Novo Nordisk's Ozempic in head-to-head diabetes trials on both blood sugar reduction and weight loss, positioning it as a major growth driver. On the technology front, a STAT investigation reveals that Epic's dominant market position is actively stifling health-tech innovation through selective data access restrictions, raising questions about whether Epic's practices unfairly disadvantage competitor AI and software developers. Several AI companies are also facing legal and ethical pressure, including OpenAI being sued over dangerous medication advice from ChatGPT, underscoring ongoing concerns about responsible AI deployment in healthcare. Meanwhile, health systems continue adapting to operational pressures, from managing Epic transitions to implementing retention strategies as Medicaid work requirements intensify.",
